            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>



&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Overall, your house has a moderate amount of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed at the windows, the weather stripping along the doors, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). I also noticed some air leakage around your attic hatch.



I did not notice a significant amount of air leakage at your range hood fan, but I recommend you replace this with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model because it was not functioning well during my site visit. 



Other upgrades recommended in this report could also improve your air leakage. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $145&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.8GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <windows>

&lt;b&gt;Windows:&lt;/b&gt;



As all of your windows are older windows that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I have modelled triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Window replacements are expensive and have a long payback period. Therefore, I do not recommend this upgrade unless there are other reasons to replace the windows, such as physical damage. However, I would recommend you consider replacing your patio door as it is an older sliding patio door, and these are often quite leaky. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $220&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</windows>
                <doors />
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Drain Water Heat Recovery:&lt;/b&gt;



A drain-water heat-recovery (DWHR) system provides a simple, maintenance-free way to reclaim some of the heat primarily from shower-water lost down the drain. These systems can save up to 40 per cent on water heating costs.



In a conventional system, cold water enters a hot water heater where it is turned into hot water.  When someone showers, the hot water proceeds from the heater to the shower where it is then lost down the drain. In a DWHR system, cold water first runs through copper coils wound tightly around the vertical plumbing drain that serves the shower. Copper is an excellent heat conductor that conducts the warmth of the drain water to the cold water in the coils without mixing the water sources.  As the cold water continues to wind up through the coils around the plumbing drain, it becomes pre-heated.  The pre-heated water continues to the hot water heater. Alternatively, the preheated water can be diverted to the cold-water line that also serves the shower.  The amount of hot water required from the water tank can then be reduced.



&lt;b&gt;Recommendation:&lt;/b&gt;

Since both your bathrooms are on the second floor, I recommend that you install a DWHR system, certified to be 42% efficient or greater, in your home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a mid-efficiency DWHR system connected to both your domestic hot water heater and your showers, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables>

&lt;b&gt;Solar Upgrade:&lt;/b&gt;



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $850&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

&lt;b&gt;Water Conservation:&lt;/b&gt;



I recommend you replace 1 toilet. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Heating - Pellet Stove:&lt;/b&gt;



At your request I have modelled the addition of a Harman P43 pellet stove to your main floor. This stove is EPA certified and has an estimated efficiency of 70%.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a pellet stove with sealed combustion and with an efficiency of 70% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $650&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.5GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps:&lt;/b&gt;



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;





ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

